Libraries like JSON Schema and Cerberus (Python) are utilized to validate data structures by defining schemas that outline the expected format, types, and constraints of the data. They allow automation scripts to ensure that the input data adheres to specified rules before further processing.

### JSON Schema Example:
```json
{
  "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "name": {
      "type": "string"
    },
    "age": {
      "type": "integer",
      "minimum": 0
    },
    "email": {
      "type": "string",
      "format": "email"
    }
  },
  "required": ["name", "age", "email"]
}
```

### Cerberus Example:
```python
from cerberus import Validator

schema = {
    'name': {'type': 'string'},
    'age': {'type': 'integer', 'min': 0},
    'email': {'type': 'string', 'regex': '^[\w\.-]+@[\w\.-]+\.\w+$'}
}

v = Validator(schema)

document = {
    'name': 'John Doe',
    'age': 29,
    'email': 'john.doe@example.com'
}

if v.validate(document):
    print("Valid data")
else:
    print("Invalid data", v.errors)
```

Both libraries provide mechanisms to catch errors and provide feedback on data validity, thus enhancing the robustness of automation scripts that depend on structured data inputs.

