In a network configuration management scenario, imagine a situation where you need to configure multiple routers in a branch office. Each router may belong to a different department with varying VLAN configurations based on department-specific needs. 

```jinja
{% set departments = ['HR', 'Sales', 'Engineering'] %}
{% set vlans = {'HR': 10, 'Sales': 20, 'Engineering': 30} %}

{% for department in departments %}
interface GigabitEthernet0/0
 description {{ department }} department interface
 switchport access vlan {{ vlans[department] }}
{% if department == 'HR' %}
 ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0
{% elif department == 'Sales' %}
 ip address 192.168.2.1 255.255.255.0
{% elif department == 'Engineering' %}
 ip address 192.168.3.1 255.255.255.0
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
```

In this template, loops and conditionals are used to dynamically generate the configuration for each department's router interface. The loop iterates over each department, while conditionals apply specific IP addresses tailored to each department’s LAN requirements. This ensures efficiency by avoiding repetitive manual entries and allowing for easy scaling as more departments may need to be added in the future.

