To limit the scope of multicast traffic and prevent specific groups from flowing out an interface, a routing policy or firewall filter can be configured to control the transmission of multicast addresses. This can be accomplished by specifying deny rules for particular multicast groups within the filtering configuration. For example, in a firewall filter, deny entries can be added for specific multicast IP ranges to block those packets from being forwarded out of the interface. Below is a JSON representation of a sample firewall filter configuration that achieves this:

```json
{
  "firewallFilter": {
    "name": "restrict-multicast",
    "terms": [
      {
        "name": "deny-multicast-group-224.0.0.1",
        "from": {
          "sourceAddress": "224.0.0.1/32"
        },
        "then": {
          "action": "deny"
        }
      },
      {
        "name": "deny-multicast-group-239.255.255.255",
        "from": {
          "sourceAddress": "239.255.255.255/32"
        },
        "then": {
          "action": "deny"
        }
      },
      {
        "name": "allow-all",
        "then": {
          "action": "accept"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

In this example, multicast packets corresponding to the groups 224.0.0.1 and 239.255.255.255 are explicitly denied, while all other traffic is accepted, thus effectively limiting the scope of multicast traffic on the interface.

