OAuth 2.0 scopes can be utilized with TM Forum Open APIs to define specific access rights for client applications. By assigning different scopes to various API endpoints, developers can control the actions that applications are allowed to perform. For example, an API may have scopes that permit read-only access to customer data or full access to modify account settings. This granularity ensures that clients only receive the permissions necessary for their functions, enhancing the security posture of the API ecosystem.

Here’s an example of how scopes might be structured in a TM Forum Open API setting:

```json
{
  "scopes": {
    "customer_read": {
      "description": "Allows read access to customer details",
      "permissions": [
        "GET /customers",
        "GET /customers/{id}"
      ]
    },
    "customer_write": {
      "description": "Allows write access to customer details",
      "permissions": [
        "POST /customers",
        "PUT /customers/{id}",
        "DELETE /customers/{id}"
      ]
    },
    "order_read": {
      "description": "Allows read access to order details",
      "permissions": [
        "GET /orders",
        "GET /orders/{id}"
      ]
    },
    "order_write": {
      "description": "Allows write access to order details",
      "permissions": [
        "POST /orders",
        "PUT /orders/{id}",
        "DELETE /orders/{id}"
      ]
    }
  }
}
``` 

In this example, each scope is associated with specific permissions granted to the client application, enabling a structured and secure authorization model.

