HATEOAS (Hypermedia as the Engine of Application State) is a key constraint of the REST application architecture that enables clients to interact with a network application entirely through hypermedia provided dynamically by the application server. This means that instead of hardcoding URLs in the client, the client utilizes hyperlinks in responses to navigate through the application states.

In TM Forum API responses, HATEOAS can be illustrated through a structured representation that includes links to related resources. Here is how it might appear:

```json
{
  "data": {
    "id": "123",
    "type": "resource",
    "attributes": {
      "name": "Sample Resource",
      "status": "active"
    },
    "links": {
      "self": {
        "href": "https://api.tmforum.org/v1/resources/123"
      },
      "related": {
        "href": "https://api.tmforum.org/v1/resources/123/related"
      },
      "update": {
        "href": "https://api.tmforum.org/v1/resources/123/update"
      },
      "delete": {
        "href": "https://api.tmforum.org/v1/resources/123/delete"
      }
    }
  }
}
``` 

In this example, the `links` object provides URIs that allow the client to perform actions and navigate through the application based on the current resource state.

